THE HUMAN POSSIBILITY SPACE

A Formal-Computational Framework for Exploring Viable Sociocultural Configurations Beyond the Historical Record

Draft V0.5 — Theoretical Foundation, Mathematical Core, and Technical Specification

Authors: Human + Machine Collective


Abstract

We develop a formal theory of the sociocultural possibility space \mathcal{P}, the set of configurations of cooperation, kinship, information, resource allocation, decision-making, power, network topology, and technological substrate that are combinatorially accessible to Homo sapiens. We distinguish the historically documented subset \mathcal{H}_{\text{obs}} \subset \mathcal{P} from the viable subset \mathcal{S} \subseteq \mathcal{P}, and define the central scientific object as the difference \mathcal{S} \setminus \mathcal{H}_{\text{obs}}. We introduce a viability vector \mathbf{V}(s) \in [0,1]^k replacing binary viability, a transition cost function c: \mathcal{P} \times \mathcal{P} \to \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0} grounded in supermodular game theory, and a technology-induced accessibility hypothesis formalizing when technological change expands \mathcal{S}. We prove a sparsity lower bound (Theorem 4.1), an institutional lock-in theorem (Theorem 6.2), and a shortcut existence condition (Theorem 6.4). We specify a six-stage computational pipeline—Reconstruct, Generate, Constraint-check, Simulate, Evolve, Analogize—and provide a fully executable ontology, extraction protocol, Bayesian occupancy model, and hybrid agent-based simulation architecture. The framework is falsifiable, and we specify the first measurable result on the \mathrm{R} \times \mathrm{D} \times \mathrm{P} subcube.

Keywords: cultural evolution, possibility space, institutional complementarity, evolutionary game theory, Bayesian occupancy, LLM-assisted hypothesis generation, agent-based simulation, path dependence.

---

1. Introduction

1.1 Motivation

The empirical record of human sociocultural organization is rich but finite. Ethnographic databases such as D-PLACE (Kirby et al. 2016) and Seshat (Turchin et al. 2015) document on the order of 10^3 societies and polities. Yet the combinatorial space of institutional configurations—mechanisms of cooperation, kinship, information transmission, resource allocation, decision aggregation, power distribution, network topology, and technological substrate—is vastly larger. The historically observed record \mathcal{H}_{\text{obs}} is therefore a biased sample of a larger viable set \mathcal{S}, which is itself a subset of the combinatorially accessible space \mathcal{P}.

The central scientific question of this program is:

What is the structure of \mathcal{S} \setminus \mathcal{H}_{\text{obs}}, the region of viable sociocultural configurations that have never been historically realized?

This question is not normative. It is structural. It asks whether the historical trajectory of Homo sapiens occupies the full viable space, or only a proper subset, and if the latter, what mechanisms—ecological, cognitive, technological—delimit the boundary.

1.2 Contributions

This monograph makes the following contributions:

1. Formal ontology of the possibility space \mathcal{P} as a constrained product of eight dimensions, with explicit compatibility relation C \subseteq \mathcal{P} correcting the Cartesian overcounting of prior drafts.
2. Observation model treating \mathcal{H}_{\text{obs}} as a biased sample with a Bayesian occupancy likelihood, distinguishing true absence from missing data.
3. Viability vector \mathbf{V}(s) = (V_{\text{bio}}, V_{\text{cog}}, V_{\text{eco}}, V_{\text{coop}}, V_{\text{strat}}, V_{\text{dyn}}, V_{\text{res}}) with formal definitions for each component.
4. Dynamical theory grounding V_{\text{coop}} and V_{\text{strat}} in evolutionary game theory, with explicit replicator dynamics, stochastic stability, and invasion probability.
5. Transition cost theory with supermodular payoff structure, proving institutional lock-in (Theorem 6.2) and shortcut existence (Theorem 6.4).
6. Technology-induced accessibility as a formal hypothesis with a testable predicate.
7. Computational architecture with six executable stages and a complete ontology specification for the \mathrm{R} \times \mathrm{D} \times \mathrm{P} subcube.
8. Falsification framework with six explicit criteria under which the central hypothesis is rejected.

1.3 Relation to Prior Work

The framework synthesizes: cultural evolution theory (Boyd & Richerson 1985; Henrich 2016), institutional complementarity (Milgrom & Roberts 1990; Aoki 2001), Ostrom's design principles for common-pool resources (Ostrom 1990), evolutionary game theory (Maynard Smith 1982; Nowak 2006), LLM-assisted hypothesis generation in social science (Argyle et al. 2023; Grossmann et al. 2023), and large-scale agent-based simulation (Park et al. 2023; Light Society 2024). It differs from prior work in treating the unobserved as a first-class scientific object rather than a residual.

