2. Formal Framework

2.1 The Possibility Space

Definition 2.1 (Dimension). A dimension D_i is a finite set of discrete modalities \{m_{i1}, \ldots, m_{ik_i}\}. We fix k_i = 6 for the initial specification, yielding |D_i| = 6 for all i.

Definition 2.2 (Raw Possibility Space). The raw possibility space is the Cartesian product

\mathcal{P}_0 = D_C \times D_K \times D_I \times D_R \times D_D \times D_P \times D_N \times D_T

where the subscripts denote Cooperation, Kinship, Information, Resource allocation, Decision aggregation, Power, Network topology, and Technology. With |D_i| = 6,

|\mathcal{P}_0| = 6^8 = 1{,}679{,}616.

Definition 2.3 (Compatibility Relation). A compatibility relation is a subset

C \subseteq \mathcal{P}_0

of coherent configurations. A configuration s \in \mathcal{P}_0 is coherent if no pair of its modalities is mutually contradictory.

Definition 2.4 (Possibility Space). The possibility space is

\mathcal{P} = \{ s \in \mathcal{P}_0 : C(s) = 1 \}.

Remark 2.5. The Cartesian product \mathcal{P}_0 overcounts because it admits incoherent combinations. For example:

· R_6 (central allocation) with D_5 (market/price) is internally contradictory unless a dual-track mechanism is specified.
· I_1 (oral) with T_6 (digital/open protocol) requires a translation substrate and is not directly realizable.
· P_5 (centralized sovereign) with D_1 (consensus) is unstable absent a federated structure.

The compatibility relation C must therefore be specified empirically or analytically. We return to this in §3.5 and Appendix A.

2.2 The Historical Record

Definition 2.6 (Historical Record). Let \mathcal{H}_{\text{obs}} \subseteq \mathcal{P} be the set of configurations documented in cross-cultural databases (D-PLACE, Seshat) after harmonization and coding.

Definition 2.7 (Observation Bias). \mathcal{H}_{\text{obs}} is not a random sample of \mathcal{P}. It is subject to:

1. Survival bias: societies that persisted are more likely documented.
2. Contact bias: societies contacted by literate observers are more likely documented.
3. Coding bias: D-PLACE and Seshat variables reflect Western anthropological categories.
4. Temporal bias: the ethnographic present (post-1800) dominates D-PLACE; Seshat covers 10,000 BCE–1900 CE but with polity-level granularity.

Definition 2.8 (True Historical Set). Let \mathcal{H}_{\text{true}} \subseteq \mathcal{P} be the set of configurations that actually existed. Then \mathcal{H}_{\text{obs}} \subseteq \mathcal{H}_{\text{true}} \subseteq \mathcal{P}, and generally \mathcal{H}_{\text{obs}} \neq \mathcal{H}_{\text{true}}.

2.3 The Unexplored Space

Definition 2.9 (Unexplored Space). The unexplored space is

\mathcal{U} = \mathcal{P} \setminus \mathcal{H}_{\text{obs}}.

We partition \mathcal{U} into three epistemically distinct regions:

\mathcal{U} = \mathcal{U}_{\text{unknown}} \;\cup\; \mathcal{U}_{\text{historically tested}} \;\cup\; \mathcal{U}_{\text{counterfactual}}

where:

· \mathcal{U}_{\text{unknown}}: configurations that may have existed but are poorly documented.
· \mathcal{U}_{\text{historically tested}}: configurations that existed but were not recorded.
· \mathcal{U}_{\text{counterfactual}}: configurations that never existed.

Remark 2.10. The central scientific object is not \mathcal{U} but the viable unexplored region:

\mathcal{U}^* = \mathcal{S} \setminus \mathcal{H}_{\text{obs}}

where \mathcal{S} \subseteq \mathcal{P} is the viable set defined in §3.

