4. Dynamical Theory

4.1 Games Induced by Configurations

Definition 4.1 (Induced Game). Each configuration s \in \mathcal{P} induces a game

G(s) = \bigl( N, \Sigma(s), u(\cdot \mid s) \bigr)

where:

· N = \{1, \ldots, n\} is the set of agents.
· \Sigma(s) = \{\sigma_1, \ldots, \sigma_m\} is the strategy set available under s.
· u: \Sigma(s) \times \Sigma(s) \to \mathbb{R} is the payoff function.

Definition 4.2 (Cooperation Game). For the \mathrm{R} \times \mathrm{D} \times \mathrm{P} subcube, we specialize to a public-goods game with punishment:

\Sigma(s) = \{C, D, P\}

where C = cooperate, D = defect, P = punish. Payoffs:

u(C, C \mid s) = b - c, \quad u(D, C \mid s) = b, \quad u(C, D \mid s) = -c

u(D, D \mid s) = 0, \quad u(P, D \mid s) = b - c_p(s), \quad u(P, P \mid s) = -c_p(s)

where:

· b: benefit of cooperation.
· c: cost of cooperation.
· c_p(s): cost of punishment, parameterized by s.

Remark 4.3. The cost c_p(s) is a function of the configuration. For example:

· R_2 (regulated commons) and P_2 (rotating recallable) reduce c_p.
· R_5 (private alienable) increases the defection payoff b.
· D_1 (consensus) increases detection probability p_{\text{detect}} but also coordination cost.

4.2 Nash and Evolutionary Stability

Definition 4.4 (Nash Equilibrium). A strategy profile \sigma^* is a Nash equilibrium of G(s) if

u(\sigma^*, \sigma^* \mid s) \geq u(\sigma', \sigma^* \mid s) \quad \forall \sigma' \in \Sigma(s).

Definition 4.5 (Evolutionary Stable Strategy). A strategy \sigma^* is an ESS if for all \sigma' \neq \sigma^*:

u(\sigma^*, \sigma^*) > u(\sigma', \sigma^*)

or

u(\sigma^*, \sigma^*) = u(\sigma', \sigma^*) \quad \text{and} \quad u(\sigma^*, \sigma') > u(\sigma', \sigma').

4.3 Replicator Dynamics

Definition 4.6. Let x_i(t) be the fraction of the population playing strategy i. The replicator dynamics are

\dot{x}_i = x_i \bigl[ u_i(x) - \bar{u}(x) \bigr]

where u_i(x) = \sum_j x_j u(\sigma_i, \sigma_j \mid s) and \bar{u}(x) = \sum_i x_i u_i(x).

Definition 4.7 (Invasion Probability). Let x^* = e_{\sigma^*} be a monomorphic population at ESS \sigma^*. Introduce a mutant \sigma' at frequency \epsilon = 0.01. Define

\rho_{\text{invade}}(\sigma', \sigma^* \mid s) = \mathbb{P}\!\left( \lim_{t \to \infty} x_{\sigma'}(t) = 1 \right).

Definition 4.8 (Cooperative Viability). The cooperative viability is

V_{\text{coop}}(s) = 1 - \mathbb{E}_{\sigma' \sim \Sigma_{\text{defect}}}\!\left[ \rho_{\text{invade}}(\sigma', \sigma^* \mid s) \right].

Definition 4.9 (Strategic Viability). The strategic viability is

V_{\text{strat}}(s) = \mathbb{P}\!\left( \sigma^* \text{ is stochastically stable under mutation and drift} \right).

4.4 Stochastic Stability

Definition 4.10. Let \mu > 0 be a mutation rate. The Wright–Fisher process with mutation has stationary distribution \pi_\mu on \Sigma(s). The stochastically stable strategies are those in the support of \lim_{\mu \to 0} \pi_\mu.

Theorem 4.11 (Young 1993). In a symmetric 2 \times 2 coordination game with mutation, the stochastically stable equilibrium is the risk-dominant one.

Remark 4.12. For n-player games with punishment, stochastic stability is determined by the balance of payoff-dominance and risk-dominance, modulated by c_p(s). Configurations with low c_p(s) (e.g., R_2, P_2, D_1) tend to stabilize cooperation.

