5. Trajectory Theory

5.1 Trajectories

Definition 5.1 (Trajectory). A trajectory is a finite sequence

\tau = (s_0, s_1, \ldots, s_T)

with s_i \in \mathcal{P} for all i.

Definition 5.2 (Transition Cost). The transition cost between s_i and s_{i+1} is

c(s_i, s_{i+1}) = c_{\text{innov}} + c_{\text{coord}} + c_{\text{conflict}} + c_{\text{learn}} + c_{\text{trans}} + \gamma(s_i, s_{i+1})

where:

· c_{\text{innov}}: cost of innovating the new modalities.
· c_{\text{coord}}: coordination cost among agents.
· c_{\text{conflict}}: cost of resolving distributional conflict.
· c_{\text{learn}}: learning cost.
· c_{\text{trans}}: transition/infrastructure cost.
· \gamma(s_i, s_{i+1}): complementarity penalty, defined in §6.

Definition 5.3 (Cumulative Cost). The cumulative cost of \tau is

L(\tau) = \sum_{i=0}^{T-1} c(s_i, s_{i+1}).

5.2 Supermodularity and Institutional Complementarity

Definition 5.4 (Supermodularity). A payoff function U: \mathcal{P} \to \mathbb{R} is supermodular in (R, D, P) if for all R \leq R', D \leq D':

U(R', D') - U(R, D') \geq U(R', D) - U(R, D).

Definition 5.5 (Institutional Complementarity). Mechanisms R_i and D_j are complementary if

\frac{\partial^2 U}{\partial R \partial D} > 0.

Definition 5.6 (Complementarity Penalty). The complementarity penalty is

\gamma(s_i, s_{i+1}) = \sum_{k < l} \gamma_{kl} \, \mathbb{1}\!\left\{ s_i^{(k)} \neq s_{i+1}^{(k)}, \, s_i^{(l)} \neq s_{i+1}^{(l)} \right\}

where \gamma_{kl} > 0 if dimensions k and l are complementary, and \gamma_{kl} < 0 if substitutable.

5.3 Lock-In Theorem

Theorem 6.2 (Institutional Lock-In). Let U be supermodular in (R, D, P). Let s_A = (R_A, D_A, P_A) and s_B = (R_B, D_B, P_B) be two configurations with R_B > R_A, D_B > D_A, P_B > P_A, and U(s_B) > U(s_A). Then the one-dimensional transition path

s_A \to (R_B, D_A, P_A) \to (R_B, D_B, P_A) \to s_B

strictly decreases U at each step whenever the complementarity penalty exceeds the direct gain.

Proof. By supermodularity, for R_B > R_A:

U(R_B, D_A) - U(R_A, D_A) \leq U(R_B, D_B) - U(R_A, D_B).

But this does not yet imply U(R_B, D_A) < U(R_A, D_A). We require the additional condition

U(R_B, D_A, P_A) < U(R_A, D_A, P_A).

This holds when the direct gain from increasing R alone is outweighed by the loss of complementarity with D_A and P_A. Formally, define the complementarity deficit

\Delta_{RD} = \bigl[ U(R_B, D_B) - U(R_B, D_A) \bigr] - \bigl[ U(R_A, D_B) - U(R_A, D_A) \bigr] > 0.

Then

U(R_B, D_A) = U(R_A, D_A) + \Delta_R - \Delta_{RD}

where \Delta_R = U(R_B, D_B) - U(R_A, D_B). If \Delta_R < \Delta_{RD}, then U(R_B, D_A) < U(R_A, D_A). The intermediate state is a valley in the payoff landscape, and the transition is blocked despite U(s_B) > U(s_A). \square

Corollary 6.3. Supermodular institutional configurations exhibit path dependence: the set of reachable configurations from s_A under local search is a sublattice of \mathcal{P} that may exclude s_B even when s_B is globally superior.

5.4 The Historical Shortcut

Definition 5.5 (Historical Shortcut). Let Q: \mathcal{P} \to \mathbb{R} be a collective capability function. A trajectory \tau_X: s_0 \to s_X \to s_E is a shortcut relative to \tau_H: s_0 \to s_1 \to \cdots \to s_E if:

1. Q(s_E) \geq Q(s_E) (equivalent or greater capability).
2. L(\tau_X) < L(\tau_H) (lower cumulative cost).

Theorem 6.4 (Shortcut Existence). If there exists s_X \in \mathcal{P} such that

c(s_0, s_X) + c(s_X, s_E) < \sum_{i=0}^{T-1} c(s_i, s_{i+1})

and Q(s_E) \geq Q_{\min}, then a shortcut exists.

Proof. Direct by Definition 5.5. \square

Remark 6.5. The term "shortcut" denotes reduced transition cost, not superior social outcome. This distinction is essential (Axiom 1, §11).

5.5 Technology-Induced Accessibility

Definition 5.6. Let T_{\text{old}}, T_{\text{new}} \in D_T. The configuration s is technology-induced accessible if

V(s \mid T_{\text{old}}) < \theta \quad \text{and} \quad V(s \mid T_{\text{new}}) \geq \theta.

Hypothesis H3 (Technology-Induced Accessibility). There exists s \in \mathcal{P} and T_{\text{old}}, T_{\text{new}} \in D_T such that s is technology-induced accessible.

Remark 5.7. H3 is the central mechanism through which AI and digital protocols may expand \mathcal{S}. It is falsifiable: if no such s exists after exhaustive search over the \mathrm{R} \times \mathrm{D} \times \mathrm{P} subcube, H3 is rejected for that subcube.

