6. Sparsity Lower Bound

Theorem 4.1 (Sparsity Lower Bound). Let \mathcal{P}_{RDP} = D_R \times D_D \times D_P with |\mathcal{P}_{RDP}| = 216. Let M_H \in \{0, 1, ?\}^{N \times 3} be the coded matrix from D-PLACE and Seshat, where ? denotes unknown. Define

N_{\text{obs}} = |\{ s \in \mathcal{P}_{RDP} : \exists h \in \mathcal{H}_{\text{obs}}, h = s \}|.

Even after conservative imputation of all ? as potentially observed, if N_{\text{obs}} < 216, then \mathcal{U} \neq \emptyset.

Proof. By counting. If the number of distinct observed configurations is strictly less than the total number of cells, at least 216 - N_{\text{obs}} cells are unobserved. Conservative imputation can only increase N_{\text{obs}}; if it remains below 216, the unobserved set is non-empty. \square

Corollary 6.1. If after Bayesian imputation with 95% credible intervals N_{\text{obs}} < 216 with probability > 0.95, then H1 (Sparsity) is supported for the RDP subcube.

Remark 6.2. The power of Theorem 4.1 is empirical, not mathematical. The theorem is trivial; the scientific content lies in the estimation of N_{\text{obs}} under an explicit observation model.

