7. Bayesian Observation Model

7.1 Occupancy Likelihood

Definition 7.1. For each cell s \in \mathcal{P}_{RDP}, define the occupancy indicator Z(s) \in \{0, 1\} and the observation indicator O(s) \in \{0, 1\}. The observation model is

\mathbb{P}(O(s) = 1 \mid Z(s) = 1) = p_{\text{detect}}(s)

\mathbb{P}(O(s) = 1 \mid Z(s) = 0) = 0

where p_{\text{detect}}(s) is the detection probability, modeled as a function of documentation intensity, geographic region, and temporal coverage.

7.2 Hierarchical Prior

Definition 7.2. The occupancy probability is modeled hierarchically:

Z(s) \sim \text{Bernoulli}(\psi(s))

\text{logit}(\psi(s)) = \alpha + \beta^\top X(s)

where X(s) is a vector of covariates (compatibility, complexity, resource requirements).

7.3 Posterior Occupancy

Definition 7.3. The posterior occupancy probability is

\pi(s) = \mathbb{P}(Z(s) = 1 \mid \{O(s')\}_{s' \in \mathcal{P}_{RDP}}).

Definition 7.4. The conservative observed count is

N_{\text{obs}}^{0.95} = |\{ s : \pi(s) > 0.95 \}|.

Remark 7.5. Theorem 4.1 applies with N_{\text{obs}} replaced by N_{\text{obs}}^{0.95}. This gives a statistically defensible sparsity claim rather than a raw count.

