10. Falsification Framework

10.1 Central Falsification Criteria

The central hypothesis is weakened or rejected if:

1. Sampling artifact: Systematic reconstruction shows the apparent unexplored space largely disappears after correcting for missing data. Formally, N_{\text{obs}}^{0.95} \to 216.
2. Analogy dominance: Most apparently novel configurations have historical analogues. Formally, A(s) > 0.6 for > 80\% of S_{\text{cand}}.
3. Constraint failure: Generated configurations overwhelmingly violate biological or ecological constraints. Formally, |\{s : V_{\text{bio}}(s) = 0 \lor V_{\text{eco}}(s) = 0\}| / |S_{\text{cand}}| > 0.8.
4. Dynamical collapse: Apparently viable novel configurations systematically collapse under evolutionary dynamics. Formally, V_{\text{coop}}(s) < \theta_{\text{coop}} for all s \in S_{\text{viable}}.
5. Technology invariance: Technological changes do not materially expand the set of viable configurations. Formally, for all s, V(s \mid T_{\text{old}}) \geq \theta \iff V(s \mid T_{\text{new}}) \geq \theta.
6. Coverage saturation: Historical trajectories adequately cover the viable region once sampling and coding uncertainty are incorporated. Formally, \mathcal{S} \setminus \mathcal{H}_{\text{obs}} \approx \emptyset.

10.2 Boxed Principles

\boxed{\text{Novelty itself is not evidence of validity.}}

\boxed{\text{Simulation survival is not evidence of real-world viability.}}

\boxed{\mathcal{U} = \text{plausible} \neq \mathcal{U} = \text{desirable}.}

10.3 Research Hypotheses

H1 (Sparsity). \mathcal{H}_{\text{obs}} \subsetneq \mathcal{S}.

H2 (Incompleteness). |\mathcal{H}_{\text{obs}} \cap \mathcal{S}| / |\mathcal{S}| < 1.

H3 (Technology-Induced Accessibility). \exists s : V(s \mid T_{\text{old}}) < \theta \land V(s \mid T_{\text{new}}) \geq \theta.

