12. Pilot Experiment: \mathrm{R} \times \mathrm{D} \times \mathrm{P}

12.1 Definition

\mathcal{P}_{RDP} = D_R \times D_D \times D_P, \quad |\mathcal{P}_{RDP}| = 6^3 = 216.

12.2 First Measurable Result

N_H = |\mathcal{H}_{\text{obs}} \cap \mathcal{P}_{RDP}|

must be empirically estimated after harmonizing D-PLACE and Seshat variables.

12.3 Research Tasks

Task Description
1. Ontology Define six operational modalities for R, D, P with formal definitions, inclusion/exclusion criteria, measurable indicators, historical examples, and uncertainty codes.
2. Reconstruction Extract R/D/P variables from D-PLACE and Seshat. Construct M_H \in \{0,1,?\}^{N \times 3}.
3. Combination Map Construct the 216-cell cube with each cell labeled (observed, partial, unknown).
4. Transition Cost Estimate c_R, c_D, c_P and interaction costs c_{RD}, c_{RP}, c_{DP}. Test for institutional complementarity and lock-in.
5. Candidate Generation Generate n = 20 initial candidates from cells with strong evidence of non-observation.
6. Constraint Testing Calculate \mathbf{V}(s) for each candidate.
7. Simulation Run repeated ABM. Candidate advances only if robust across seeds and parameter ranges.

12.4 Success Criterion

Find s_{\text{blue}} \in \mathcal{P}_{RDP} \setminus \mathcal{H}_{\text{obs}} such that:

1. V_{\text{bio}}(s_{\text{blue}}) = 1, V_{\text{eco}}(s_{\text{blue}}) = 1 (hard constraints).
2. V_{\text{coop}}(s_{\text{blue}}) > 0.8 across 10 seeds (stable).
3. A(s_{\text{blue}}) = \max_h \text{sim}(s_{\text{blue}}, h) < 0.6 (truly novel).

If one such s_{\text{blue}} is found, H1 and H2 survive the first test. If 0/20 survive, H1 is weakened.

12.5 Failure Criterion

All candidates fail at least one stage, or stability is highly seed-dependent.

---

13. Limitations

1. Ontology overlap: Several modalities are not orthogonal (e.g., C_5 with P; I_5 with T_6; D_5 with R_5; N conflates topology and scale). The ontology requires empirical validation via inter-coder reliability.
2. Compatibility relation: C is not fully specified; the cardinality |\mathcal{P}| is currently estimated, not derived.
3. Threshold calibration: \boldsymbol{\theta} requires empirical or expert-derived values; sensitivity analysis is required.
4. Game specification: Nash and ESS conditions are written for symmetric two-player games; many configurations involve n-player public goods with asymmetric roles.
5. Transition costs: c_{\text{innovation}}, c_{\text{coordination}}, c_{\text{conflict}}, c_{\text{learning}}, c_{\text{transition}} require operationalization via historical case studies and expert elicitation.
6. LLM reliability: LLM-generated candidates may be fluent but historically shallow or biased; multi-LLM, preregistration, and blinding are required.
7. ABM validity: LLM-powered agent simulations are not yet validated for real-world sociocultural viability; hybrid approaches with rule-based agents are recommended.
8. Data bias: D-PLACE and Seshat are biased by documentation, survival, contact, and coding schemes; the Bayesian occupancy model partially corrects but does not eliminate this.

---

14. Conclusion

We have developed a formal, falsifiable, and computationally executable framework for exploring the human sociocultural possibility space beyond the historical record. The central scientific object is \mathcal{S}_{\text{viable}} \setminus \mathcal{H}_{\text{observed}}, the region of viable configurations outside the historically documented trajectory. The framework integrates:

· A constrained possibility space \mathcal{P} with compatibility relation C.
· A viability vector \mathbf{V}(s) \in [0,1]^7.
· A dynamical theory grounding cooperation and stability in evolutionary game theory.
· A trajectory theory with supermodular lock-in and shortcut existence.
· A Bayesian observation model distinguishing true absence from missing data.
· A six-stage computational pipeline with executable ontology, extraction, and simulation specifications.
· A falsification framework with six explicit criteria.

The next iteration should produce:

1. The complete ontology JSON for R, D, P (R is complete; D and P pending).
2. The D-PLACE/Seshat extraction script.
3. The first run of the RDP pilot.
4. Empirical calibration of \boldsymbol{\theta}.
5. Inter-coder reliability assessment (\kappa \geq 0.6).

The framework is designed to fail if the historical trajectory adequately covers the viable region. Its scientific value lies in being able to make that failure informative.

THE HUMAN POSSIBILITY SPACE

