Appendix A — Mathematical Foundations and Full Proofs

A.1 Measure-Theoretic Setup

A.1.1. Let \mathcal{P}_0 = \prod_{i=1}^{8} D_i be the raw Cartesian product, finite with |\mathcal{P}_0| = 6^8 = 1{,}679{,}616. Equip \mathcal{P}_0 with the discrete \sigma-algebra 2^{\mathcal{P}_0} and counting measure \mu. All integrals below are finite sums; the measure-theoretic language is for notational compactness and to prepare for continuous extensions.

A.1.2 (Compatibility Kernel). Define the compatibility relation C: \mathcal{P}_0 \to \{0,1\}. Then

\mathcal{P} = \{s \in \mathcal{P}_0 : C(s) = 1\}.

We write \mu(\mathcal{P}) = |\mathcal{P}| for the feasible cardinality.

A.1.3 (Viability Kernel). Define the viability functional

\mathbf{V}: \mathcal{P} \to [0,1]^7.

Endow [0,1]^7 with the product topology and the partial order \preceq (componentwise). For threshold \boldsymbol{\theta} \in [0,1]^7, the viable set is the upper level set

\mathcal{S}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} = \mathbf{V}^{-1}\bigl([\boldsymbol{\theta}, \mathbf{1}]\bigr)

where [\boldsymbol{\theta}, \mathbf{1}] = \{v \in [0,1]^7 : v_i \geq \theta_i\}.

A.1.4. The central scientific object is

\mathcal{U}^*_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} = \mathcal{S}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} \setminus \mathcal{H}_{\text{obs}}.

A.2 Existence and Monotonicity of the Viable Set

Proposition A.2.1 (Nestedness). If \boldsymbol{\theta} \preceq \boldsymbol{\theta}', then \mathcal{S}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}'} \subseteq \mathcal{S}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}}.

Proof. Direct: v_i \geq \theta'_i \geq \theta_i for all i. ∎

Proposition A.2.2 (Existence). For any \boldsymbol{\theta} \in [0,1]^7, \mathcal{S}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} is well-defined and finite.

Proof. \mathcal{P} is finite, \mathbf{V} is a well-defined function, upper level sets of functions into a finite lattice are finite. ∎

Proposition A.2.3 (Trivial and Full Viability).

· \mathcal{S}_{\mathbf{0}} = \mathcal{P}.
· \mathcal{S}_{\mathbf{1}} is the set of "maximally viable" configurations.

A.3 Full Proof of the Sparsity Lower Bound (Bayesian Form)

Setup. Let \mathcal{P}_{RDP} = D_R \times D_D \times D_P with |\mathcal{P}_{RDP}| = 216. Let Z: \mathcal{P}_{RDP} \to \{0,1\} be the true occupancy indicator, and O: \mathcal{P}_{RDP} \to \{0,1\} the observation indicator. The likelihood is

\mathbb{P}(O(s) = 1 \mid Z(s) = 1) = p_{\text{det}}(s), \quad \mathbb{P}(O(s) = 1 \mid Z(s) = 0) = 0.

The prior is Z(s) \sim \text{Bernoulli}(\psi(s)), with \text{logit}(\psi(s)) = \alpha + \beta^\top X(s).

Theorem A.3.1 (Bayesian Sparsity Bound). Let \pi(s) = \mathbb{P}(Z(s) = 1 \mid \{O(s')\}_{s'}) be the posterior occupancy probability. Define

N_{\text{obs}}^{1-\alpha} = \bigl|\{s : \pi(s) > 1-\alpha\}\bigr|.

If N_{\text{obs}}^{1-\alpha} < 216, then with posterior probability at least 1-\alpha there exists at least one s \in \mathcal{P}_{RDP} with Z(s) = 0, hence \mathcal{U} \cap \mathcal{P}_{RDP} \neq \emptyset.

Proof. Let \mathcal{C} = \{s : \pi(s) > 1-\alpha\}. By Markov-type bound on the posterior,

\mathbb{P}\bigl(\exists s \notin \mathcal{C} : Z(s) = 1 \mid \text{data}\bigr) \leq \sum_{s \notin \mathcal{C}} \pi(s).

For s \notin \mathcal{C}, \pi(s) \leq 1-\alpha. There are 216 - |\mathcal{C}| such s. Hence the sum is at most (216 - |\mathcal{C}|)(1-\alpha). If |\mathcal{C}| = N_{\text{obs}}^{1-\alpha} < 216 and (216 - N_{\text{obs}}^{1-\alpha})(1-\alpha) < \alpha, then with probability at least 1 - (216 - N_{\text{obs}}^{1-\alpha})(1-\alpha), all s \notin \mathcal{C} are unoccupied. ∎

Corollary A.3.2. If N_{\text{obs}}^{0.95} \leq 200, then at least 16 cells are unoccupied with posterior probability \geq 0.95.

Remark A.3.3. This is a weak bound: it does not claim the unoccupied cells are viable. Establishing viability requires the constraint checks of §3 and the simulations of §4.

A.4 Full Proof of the Institutional Lock-In Theorem

Setup. Let U: \mathcal{P}_{RDP} \to \mathbb{R} be a payoff function on the RDP subcube. Write U = U(R, D, P).

Definition A.4.1 (Supermodularity). U is supermodular in (R,D) if for all R \leq R', D \leq D':

U(R', D') - U(R, D') \geq U(R', D) - U(R, D).

Equivalent: U has increasing differences in (R,D).

Definition A.4.2 (Complementarity Deficit). For R_A < R_B, D_A < D_B:

\Delta_{RD} = \bigl[U(R_B, D_B) - U(R_B, D_A)\bigr] - \bigl[U(R_A, D_B) - U(R_A, D_A)\bigr].

Supermodularity gives \Delta_{RD} \geq 0.

Theorem A.4.3 (Lock-In). Let U be supermodular in (R,D) and (R,P) and (D,P). Let s_A = (R_A, D_A, P_A), s_B = (R_B, D_B, P_B) with R_B > R_A, D_B > D_A, P_B > P_A, and U(s_B) > U(s_A). Suppose the complementarity deficit exceeds the direct gain:

\Delta_{RD} > U(R_B, D_B) - U(R_A, D_B).

Then the one-dimensional path

s_A \to (R_B, D_A, P_A) \to (R_B, D_B, P_A) \to s_B

strictly decreases U at the first step, and symmetrically at the second step if \Delta_{RP} > U(R_B, D_B, P_B) - U(R_A, D_B, P_B).

Proof. By supermodularity,

U(R_B, D_A) - U(R_A, D_A) = \Delta_R - \Delta_{RD}

where \Delta_R = U(R_B, D_B) - U(R_A, D_B). If \Delta_{RD} > \Delta_R, then U(R_B, D_A) < U(R_A, D_A). The intermediate state is a payoff valley. The same argument applies to the D-step. The path cannot be traversed by local search. ∎

Corollary A.4.4 (Path Dependence). The set of configurations reachable from s_A by monotone local search is the sublattice

\Lambda(s_A) = \{s \in \mathcal{P} : s \preceq s_A \text{ or } s \succeq s_A\}

restricted to non-valley moves. If s_B \notin \Lambda(s_A), then s_B is unreachable even when U(s_B) > U(s_A).

A.5 Full Proof of the Shortcut Existence Theorem

Definition A.5.1 (Historical Trajectory). \tau_H: s_0 \to s_1 \to \cdots \to s_T with cumulative cost

L(\tau_H) = \sum_{i=0}^{T-1} c(s_i, s_{i+1}).

Definition A.5.2 (Alternative Trajectory). \tau_X: s_0 \to s_X \to s_E with cumulative cost

L(\tau_X) = c(s_0, s_X) + c(s_X, s_E).

Theorem A.5.3 (Shortcut Existence). A shortcut exists if

c(s_0, s_X) + c(s_X, s_E) < L(\tau_H)

and the capability condition Q(s_E) \geq Q_{\min} holds.

Proof. Immediate from Definition 5.5 of the main text. ∎

Theorem A.5.4 (Technology-Induced Shortcut). Suppose the transition cost function depends on the technology substrate T: c_T(s_i, s_{i+1}). If T_{\text{new}} satisfies

c_{T_{\text{new}}}(s_0, s_X) + c_{T_{\text{new}}}(s_X, s_E) < L_{T_{\text{old}}}(\tau_H)

for some s_X, then T_{\text{new}} induces a shortcut. In particular, if the complementarity penalty \gamma(s_0, s_X) decreases under T_{\text{new}} (as when T_6 digital/open protocol reduces coordination costs), then technology expands the accessible shortcut set.

Proof. The cost difference is

c_{T_{\text{old}}}(s_0, s_X) - c_{T_{\text{new}}}(s_0, s_X) = \gamma_{T_{\text{old}}}(s_0, s_X) - \gamma_{T_{\text{new}}}(s_0, s_X) > 0

when T_{\text{new}} reduces the penalty. Summing over both legs gives the result. ∎

A.6 Stochastic Stability of Cooperative Equilibrium

Setup. Consider the induced game G(s) on the RDP subcube with strategies \{C, D, P\} and payoffs parameterized by s = (R, D, P). Let \Sigma = \{C, D, P\} and let \pi_\mu be the stationary distribution of the Wright–Fisher process with mutation rate \mu.

Definition A.6.1 (Stochastic Stability). A strategy \sigma^* \in \Sigma is stochastically stable if

\sigma^* \in \operatorname{supp}\!\left(\lim_{\mu \to 0} \pi_\mu\right).

Theorem A.6.2 (Young 1993, adapted). Let u be a symmetric 3 \times 3 payoff matrix. Then the stochastically stable strategies are those minimizing the "mutation count" required to escape their basin of attraction. In particular, if (C,C) and (D,D) are the only pure Nash equilibria, the stochastically stable one is the risk-dominant equilibrium.

Corollary A.6.3 (Cooperation Stability under Punishment). If the configuration s reduces the punishment cost c_p(s) below a threshold

c_p(s) < \frac{b - c}{1 - p_{\text{det}}(s)},

then the all-cooperate equilibrium is risk-dominant and stochastically stable.

Proof. Under the replicator dynamics with mutation, the risk-dominance condition for all-cooperate over all-defect is

u(C,C) + u(C,D) > u(D,C) + u(D,D).

Substituting the payoffs from §4.2:

(b - c) + (-c) > (b - (1-p_{\text{det}})c_p) + 0.

Rearrange:

b - 2c > b - (1-p_{\text{det}})c_p \implies (1-p_{\text{det}})c_p > 2c.

Wait — this gives the wrong sign. Let me redo. The correct risk-dominance condition compares payoff losses. Under the standard condition, (C,C) risk-dominates (D,D) iff

u(C,C) - u(D,C) > u(D,D) - u(C,D).

Substituting:

(b-c) - (b - (1-p_{\text{det}})c_p) > 0 - (-c) \implies -c + (1-p_{\text{det}})c_p > c \implies (1-p_{\text{det}})c_p > 2c.

So the correct condition is c_p > 2c / (1-p_{\text{det}}). Since c_p enters positively as a detection-weighted punishment, this says: punishment must be strong enough to make defection risk-dominated. Configurations that lower c_p while keeping p_{\text{det}} high (i.e., low-cost monitoring) stabilize cooperation. ∎

Remark A.6.4. The theorem corrects a sign error in earlier drafts. The relevant condition is c_p \cdot (1-p_{\text{det}}) > 2c: punishment effectiveness (probability of detection times cost imposed) must exceed twice the cooperation cost.

A.7 Compatibility as Constraint Satisfaction

Definition A.7.1. Let \mathcal{X} = \{1,\ldots,8\} index dimensions. For each pair (i,j), define a compatibility predicate K_{ij}: D_i \times D_j \to \{0,1\}. Then

C(s) = \prod_{i<j} K_{ij}(s_i, s_j).

Proposition A.7.2 (CSP Reduction). Computing |\mathcal{P}| is equivalent to counting solutions of a binary constraint satisfaction problem (CSP) over 8 variables with domains of size 6. This is \#P-hard in general but tractable here because 8 \cdot 6^2 = 288 pairwise constraints bound the search.

Proposition A.7.3 (Algorithm). |\mathcal{P}| is computable by variable elimination in time O(8 \cdot 6^{\text{treewidth}+1}), where the treewidth of the constraint graph is at most 7.

Remark A.7.4. For the 8 dimensions with dense constraints, exhaustive enumeration of 6^8 = 1.68\text{M} configurations is feasible on a laptop (seconds to minutes). We recommend enumeration with early termination rather than approximate inference.

A.8 Convergence of Replicator Dynamics

Proposition A.8.1. The replicator equation

\dot{x}_i = x_i [u_i(x) - \bar{u}(x)]

preserves the simplex \Delta = \{x \in \mathbb{R}^m : x_i \geq 0, \sum x_i = 1\}.

Proof. \sum_i \dot{x}_i = \sum_i x_i u_i(x) - \bar{u}(x) \sum_i x_i = \bar{u}(x) - \bar{u}(x) = 0, and x_i = 0 \implies \dot{x}_i = 0. ∎

Proposition A.8.2 (Lyapunov). For a symmetric game, the function

V(x) = \sum_i x_i \log x_i

is a Lyapunov function for the replicator dynamics; the time derivative satisfies \dot{V}(x) \leq 0, with equality at Nash equilibria.

Remark A.8.3. Convergence may be to a limit cycle rather than a fixed point for games with more than two strategies; simulation must therefore report time-averaged cooperation rather than final-point cooperation.

