E.2 Agent Specification

Rule-based agent. State: (\sigma, \text{memory}, \text{payoff}, \text{neighbors}). Update rule: replicator dynamics with local interaction.

LLM agent. State: (\text{persona}, \text{memory}, \text{config}). Update rule: prompt the LLM with current state and configuration, ask for strategy choice. See Appendix F.

Hybridization. Rule-based agents dominate structural dynamics (resource flow, population change). LLM agents dominate strategy updates and communication. Coupling: LLM agents observe rule-based aggregate state; rule-based agents observe LLM strategy distribution.

E.3 Environment

Resource pool. Total resource R_{\text{total}}(t). Regeneration rate r depends on R modality:

R(t+1) = R(t) + r_{R}(R(t)) - \sum_i a_i(t)

where a_i(t) is agent i's appropriation.

Interaction graph. Adjacency depends on N, I:

· N_1: complete graph on \sim 50 nodes.
· N_6: scale-free network via Barabási–Albert.
· I_1 (oral): local interactions only.
· I_5 (digital): global interactions with latency.

Institutional constraints. Enforced via payoff modulation and message filtering.

E.4 Interaction Protocol

At each generation:

1. Agents observe environment and neighbor states.
2. Agents choose strategies (rule-based: deterministic; LLM: prompted).
3. Payoffs are computed via u(\sigma_i, \sigma_j \mid s).
4. Replicator update with mutation \mu = 0.01.
5. Environmental update.
6. Metrics recorded.

E.5 Robustness Testing

For each candidate s:

· Seed sweep: 10 independent seeds.
· Parameter sweep: perturb b, c, c_p, p_{\text{det}} by \pm 20\%.
· Mutant sweep: introduce \{C, D, P\} mutants at \epsilon = 0.01.
· Network sweep: perturb graph by rewiring 10\% of edges.
· Shock sweep: at generation G/2, apply resource shock (30% reduction).

Advancement criterion: a candidate advances only if the qualitative outcome (survival, cooperation > 0.5) is preserved across ≥ 80% of perturbations.

