Appendix J — Glossary

Architecture. A configuration s = (c, k, i, r, d, p, n, t) \in \mathcal{P}.

Compatibility relation. The subset C \subseteq \mathcal{P}_0 of coherent configurations.

Complementarity penalty. \gamma(s_i, s_{i+1}), the extra cost of changing two complementary dimensions simultaneously.

Counterfactual configuration. A configuration s \in \mathcal{U}_{\text{counterfactual}} that never historically existed.

Detection probability. p_{\text{det}}(s), the probability that if s existed, it would be documented.

Historical shortcut. A trajectory \tau_X with L(\tau_X) < L(\tau_H) achieving Q \geq Q_{\min}.

Institutional complementarity. The property \partial^2 U / \partial R \partial D > 0 that makes two mechanisms mutually reinforcing.

Lock-in. The condition under which a superior configuration is unreachable by local search due to complementarity.

Occupancy. Z(s) \in \{0,1\}, whether configuration s exists.

Possibility space. \mathcal{P}, the set of coherent configurations.

Sparsity. H1: \mathcal{H}_{\text{obs}} \subsetneq \mathcal{S}.

Technology-induced accessibility. The property that a configuration is viable under T_{\text{new}} but not T_{\text{old}}.

Viability vector. \mathbf{V}(s) \in [0,1]^7, the multi-dimensional viability score.

Viable set. \mathcal{S}_{\boldsymbol{\theta}} = \{s : \mathbf{V}(s) \geq \boldsymbol{\theta}\}.

